      "Use order weight for logistics estimates only after confirming truck, trailer, floor or lifting capacity independently."
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: "How much does one cubic yard of normal-weight concrete weigh?", a: "At 150 lb/ft³, one cubic yard weighs 4,050 lb, or about 2.025 US tons. Actual concrete density can vary by mix." },
      { q: "Why is concrete density editable?", a: "Lightweight, normal-weight and heavyweight mixes can differ substantially. An editable density lets the weight estimate match the material specification instead of forcing one universal value." },
      { q: "Does waste increase the estimated weight?", a: "Yes. The calculator shows the net volume and the allowance-adjusted volume, then applies density to each so you can distinguish placed material from ordered material." },
      { q: "Can this be used for structural load design?", a: "No. It estimates material mass from volume and density. Structural dead-load combinations, capacity and code checks require project-specific engineering." }
    ]
  },
  "concrete-cost-calculator": {
    title: "Concrete Cost Calculator — Ready-Mix & Bag Cost per Yard",
    description: "Estimate concrete material cost from cubic yards or rounded bag quantities, with waste, tax, delivery, short-load, pump and other quoted project charges.",
    guidance: [
      "Match the price unit to the supplier quote: per cubic yard, cubic meter, cubic foot, bag or ton.",
      "Material tax is applied to the material subtotal; delivery, short-load, pumping and other project charges are entered separately.",
